    "paper:v17.2-normalize-field-repair-evidence-strict-1:6e3b8041ca3c503063cd1b0184e654175156de30eebb06aa8682ae37a3a4c8ae": {
      "analysis": {
        "analysis": {
          "scope_and_question": "The review examines digital health innovations, equity gaps, and governance in infectious disease surveillance, focusing on the central question of how digital health technologies contribute to pandemic preparedness.",
          "evidence_base_and_review_method": "This is a structured thematic narrative review following PRISMA 2020 and PRISMA-ScR guidelines, synthesizing peer-reviewed and grey literature from January 2010 to March 2025. PubMed, Scopus, and Web of Science were searched from February 5 to 18, 2025, using a three-concept Boolean search strategy. Records were deduplicated in EndNote X20 and screened by two blinded reviewers (Cohen κ = 0.81). Empirical studies were appraised using the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool 2018, and reviews using Joanna Briggs Institute checklists. The synthesis was performed via Braun and Clarke's reflexive thematic analysis, interpreted through sociotechnical systems and data justice lenses.",
          "consensus_and_key_conclusions": "The synthesis of 142 included studies supports the conclusion that digital health is necessary but not sufficient for pandemic preparedness. No single technology was universally superior. The evidence consistently shows a regional skew, with high-income countries contributing 41% of studies and sub-Saharan Africa or South Asia 19%. AI studies are concentrated in HICs (56%), while SMS mHealth studies are concentrated in LMICs (60%). The patterns observed indicate that equity, sustainability, and accountability are constituents of effectiveness rather than obstacles to it.",
          "controversies_and_evidence_gaps": "The evidence base is heavily skewed regionally, with significant underrepresentation from sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia. Recurring barriers include algorithmic bias, fragmented interoperability, donor-dependent sustainability, and slow benefit-sharing of pathogen data with LMICs. The binding constraint is increasingly political and institutional will rather than analytic uncertainty.",
          "research_and_practice_implications": "Future research should prioritize addressing regional disparities and the specific barriers identified, such as algorithmic bias and interoperability. Policymakers should focus on strengthening institutional will and ensuring equitable benefit-sharing of pathogen data with LMICs."
        },
        "summary_en": "This review critically examines digital health innovations, equity gaps, and governance in infectious disease surveillance, synthesizing 142 studies from 2010 to 2025. The authors find that digital health is necessary but not sufficient for pandemic preparedness, with no single technology universally superior. A significant regional skew exists, with high-income countries contributing 41% of studies and sub-Saharan Africa or South Asia only 19%. AI studies are concentrated in HICs, while SMS mHealth studies are concentrated in LMICs. Recurring barriers include algorithmic bias, fragmented interoperability, donor-dependent sustainability, and slow benefit-sharing of pathogen data with LMICs. The binding constraint is increasingly political and institutional will rather than analytic uncertainty. The review concludes that equity, sustainability, and accountability are constituents of effectiveness rather than obstacles to it.",
